{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"# Phase 1 EXIT — confirmed root causes (rebar-debug)\n\nWorktree `fix-janitor-peccary` @ origin/main. Two independent, proven mechanisms; the\nticket's two ACs map one-to-one onto them.\n\n## First: a handed-in claim is REFUTED — the janitor DOES have a caller\n\nThe investigation was handed evidence that `start_background_janitor`/`run_gc` \"matched only\n`_snapshot/janitor.py` and its re-export\", i.e. the GC is dead code. That is **false**, by both\nrequired search methods:\n\n* `grep -rn start_background_janitor` (name as a STRING) →\n  `src/rebar/review_bot/app.py:134-136` — a function-local import inside the FastAPI\n  `lifespan`, plus `infra/runbooks/review-bot-ops.md:500-501` documenting it.\n* Serena `find_referencing_symbols` (semantic) → the same site,\n  `lifespan` / `lifespan/_janitor_thread` in `src/rebar/review_bot/app.py`.\n\nBoth methods agree. The janitor is wired; **wiring it up is NOT the fix**. The defect is\nexactly what this ticket says it is: the *third* trigger the ADR promises does not exist, and\nthe dominant entry kind is not accounted.\n\n## RC-1 — the ADR-promised byte-total backstop was never implemented (omission)\n\n**Promised.** `docs/adr/0005-snapshot-cache-architecture.md:72-73` (D5): \"reclaims under a\nfree-space watermark (LRU by `mtime`, skipping a short grace window), **backstopped by the byte\ntotal** and a secondary max-age cold-trim.\" Restated verbatim as the module contract at\n`src/rebar/_snapshot/janitor.py:12-15`: \"Primary = a FREE-SPACE watermark\n(`shutil.disk_usage`), **backstopped by the incrementally-maintained byte total** (no hot-path\n`du`).\" The originating story `dodgy-luff-quasar` (b73c-648f-f44d-4ac5) says the same under\n\"What\": \"Trigger: primary = FREE-SPACE watermark ..., **backstopped by an\nincrementally-maintained byte total** (no hot-path `du`).\"\n\n**Implemented.** `_gc_pass` (`janitor.py:376-416`) reads exactly two inputs:\n`shutil.disk_usage(root).free` (via `_space_thresholds`) and each entry's `mtime` (grace +\nmax-age). It never calls `_cache.byte_total`. The ONLY caller of `byte_total` in the janitor is\n`startup_sweep` (`janitor.py:283`), which *reconciles* the counter against a walk — that is\nbookkeeping, not eviction. There is no cap knob: `_default_tunables()` (`janitor.py:86-95`),\n`resolve_janitor_tunables` (`_config_resolvers.py:170-200`) and the documented `[snapshot]`\ntable (`docs/config.md:473-484`) all carry six knobs, none of them a size cap.\n\n**Runtime proof (dynamic hypothesis — source reading would not have sufficed).** Scratch store,\n4 entries, `mtime` pushed 10,000 s past a 120 s grace window, `max_age_seconds=10**9` (cold-trim\ndisarmed), `free_bytes=10**15` against `free_watermark_bytes=1` (free-space term disarmed), and\n`byte_total` forced to **500 GiB**. The only term that could evict is a byte-total term.\n\n    E1 byte_total=500.0 GiB, entries=4\n    E1 RESULT evicted=0 reclaimed=0 skipped=None\n\n`skipped=None` confirms the flock was taken and `_gc_pass` actually executed. Zero evictions\nwith 500 GiB accounted ⇒ no byte-total trigger exists at any threshold. This also discriminates\nagainst the rivals \"the backstop exists but is clamped/misconfigured\" (500 GiB exceeds any\nplausible default cap) and \"eviction was blocked by grace or cold-trim\" (both disarmed).\n\n## RC-2 — `materialize_tickets` populates without byte-accounting (unmet obligation)\n\n**The obligation.** ADR 0005 D2 (`:47`): \"A running byte total is maintained **incrementally**\n(atomic flock read-modify-write) so the janitor never needs a hot-path `du`.\"\n`cache.acquire` meets it — its docstring promises to \"account the populated bytes exactly once\"\nand it calls `add_bytes(entry_size(dest), root)` at `src/rebar/_snapshot/cache.py:203`, on the\nbranch where it won the populate race.\n\n**The violation.** `materialize_tickets` (`repo_snapshot.py:616-710`) is a *second* populate\npath that bypasses `acquire` entirely: build → `os.rename(build, dest)` → `return str(dest)`\n(`:693-702`). No `add_bytes`. Note `janitor._is_entry` (`:150-162`) explicitly says these\nentries must not be \"invisible to GC **+ the byte total**\" — so the intent is on record in the\njanitor itself.\n\n**Runtime proof (dynamic).** `materialize_tickets` against a scratch repo, measuring\n`cache.byte_total` immediately before and after the call:\n\n    E2 entry=tickets-5e09f2e0... real_bytes=200004  byte_total 0 -> 0 (delta 0)\n\nA real 200 KB entry landed; the counter did not move. Rules out \"accounting happens lazily\nsomewhere else on the populate path\".\n\n**Scale, measured on this host's shared store** (`$TMPDIR/rebar-gate-snapshots`, read-only\ninspection — nothing deleted):\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| entries | 64,021 (34,851 `tickets-<sha>`, 27,505 code `<sha>`) |\n| a real `tickets-<sha>` entry | 881,644 KB ≈ **861 MiB** (sampled, most-recent-first) |\n| `bytes.total` reports | 50,722,852,761 B = **47.24 GiB** for the whole store |\n\nTwo current tickets entries alone are ~1.7 GiB and contribute **zero**. So even once RC-1 is\nfixed, the cap would be compared against a total that omits the largest thing in the store.\nThe two causes are genuinely independent and neither subsumes the other.\n\n## Defect legitimacy\n\n* RC-1 → `incomplete-implementation`. Cited design authority: ADR 0005 D5 and the story's\n  \"What\". Approval is not in question, so this is a defect-class miss (finish the approved\n  work), not a feature request.\n* RC-2 → `defect`. Violates the cited invariant ADR 0005 D2:47 + `acquire`'s documented\n  \"exactly once\" contract + `_is_entry`'s own stated requirement.\n\nBoth unlock Phase 2.\n\n## Caller-dependency sweep\n\n* `materialize_tickets` — one production caller, `src/rebar/llm/gate_source.py:99`; it consumes\n  the returned path string only. Every other reference is a test or a docstring. No caller reads\n  `byte_total`, so incrementing it is **incidental** to all of them, and it makes the\n  incremental counter AGREE with `startup_sweep`'s authoritative walk (which has counted these\n  entries since PR #69) rather than diverge from it.\n* The new cap knob defaults to **off**, so no existing deployment's behaviour changes.\n\nGate cleared — no behavioural dependency.\n\n## Defect origin (`caused_by`)\n\nNeither cause is findable by blame: an omission has no introducing commit, and RC-2's fix site\nhas been correct-at-the-time since it was written. Both are attributed **by scope-of-work**, and\nboth diverge from what a whole-file blame majority would name — recorded here per protocol.\n\n* **RC-1 → `dodgy-luff-quasar` / b73c-648f-f44d-4ac5** (story \"Snapshot cache janitor —\n  reclamation under disk pressure\", closed). Its \"What\" scopes the byte-total backstop in so\n  many words, yet **none of its 9 acceptance criteria mentions it** — the ACs cover the\n  free-space watermark, the max-age cold-trim, the startup-sweep reconcile, byte-total\n  consistency under concurrent populate-vs-evict, the trash straggler, rename-to-trash, the\n  gc/lock, self-heal, and the tunables. The promise and its non-implementation shipped in the\n  same commit, `b25fafcd18` (\"Repo-snapshot isolation for the code-reading gates (#57)\",\n  2026-06-27), which authored ADR 0005 D5 and `_gc_pass` together. This is the ticket whose\n  acceptance criteria the missing behaviour falls under, and its reviewer could plausibly have\n  caught the gap between the \"What\" and the AC list.\n\n* **RC-2 → `swampy-knot-mirage` / 615c-7646-4566-4bb8** (bug, closed), landed as\n  `ec85e4cd54` \"fix(plan-review): pin ticket store in gate snapshot ...\". That change introduced\n  `materialize_tickets` — a new populate path — into a store whose incremental-accounting\n  obligation had already been established by #57. The obligation predates the site, so this is\n  an unmet obligation whose *consumer* is the culprit: #57's reviewer could not have known about\n  a future producer, whereas #57's contract was on the page in front of #67's reviewer.\n\n  **Considered and rejected: `85b4-9850-a474-4bae` / `daabd4bee5`** (\"GC the pinned ticket-store\n  entries (#69)\"). Its commit message claims the tickets entries \"were invisible to eviction AND\n  the byte total\" and that it fixed both. It did not — it fixed `_is_entry`, which restored them\n  to `startup_sweep`'s reconciling walk but left the incremental path untouched. It is a\n  *partial* fix, not the origin of the omission; the omission is #67's.\n\nOnly one `caused_by` can ride the close, so the close will carry RC-1's origin (the ticket's\nheadline AC); RC-2's attribution is recorded here and linked separately."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787265561375640001,"uuid":"36b4f4e8-a6bd-453a-be0c-a92c4e7577e7"}